{"name":"Blue Veil","hex":"#aecbe5","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"veil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something hung up or spread out to hide or protect the face, or hide an object from view; usually of gauze, crepe, or similar diaphanous material.","Anything that partially obscures a clear view."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dress in, or decorate with, a veil.","To conceal as with a veil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/veil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Daughters of Divine Love","blocks":[{"text":"The Daughters of Divine Love Congregation, a Catholic pontifical and international order of religious women was founded by Bishop Godfrey Mary Paul Okoye on July 16, 1969, in Nigeria, during the Nigerian Civil War. The congregation has over 900 sisters ministering in 15 countries around the world. The members pronounce the public vows of chastity, poverty and obedience, and dedicate themselves to contemplation and apostolic work. The congregation, recognized by their blue veil, serves in the following countries:"},{"items":["Africa: Cameroon, Gabon, Kenya, Mali, Nigeria, Chad","Europe: England, Austria, Germany, Italy, Switzerland, Belgium","Americas: Cuba, Jamaica, Haiti, United States"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daughters_of_Divine_Love"},{"title":"Blue Veil (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Veil is a solo studio album by British cellist, composer, and curator Lucy Railton. It was released on 18 April 2025 through Ideologic Organ. It received universal acclaim from critics."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/cc/Blue_Veil_%28album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Veil_(album)"},{"title":"Veil","blocks":[{"text":"A veil is an article of clothing or hanging cloth that is intended to cover some part of the head or face, or an object of some significance. Veiling has a long history in European, Asian, and African societies. The practice has been prominent in different forms in Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. The practice of veiling is especially associated with women and sacred objects, though in some cultures, it is men, rather than women, who are expected to wear a veil. Besides its enduring religious significance, veiling continues to play a role in some modern secular contexts, such as wedding customs."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Chief_Vestal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veil"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg/500px-Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg/500px-Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Greek bronze statuette of a veiled and masked dancer, 2nd–3rd century BC.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Veils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg/500px-Veils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Veils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg/500px-Veils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of Isabeau of Bavaria, queen of France, wearing veiling","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVeils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/A_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._%281918%29_-_TIMEA.jpg/500px-A_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._%281918%29_-_TIMEA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/A_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._%281918%29_-_TIMEA.jpg/500px-A_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._%281918%29_-_TIMEA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coptic woman wearing a veil (1918)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._(1918)_-_TIMEA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Targui.jpg/500px-Targui.jpg?utm_sourc